in{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi [(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-{2}^{4}+\\frac{4\\cdot {2}^{3}}{3})-0] \\\\  & =4\\pi [(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{2}+\\frac{{2}^{5}}{3})-0] \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {2}^{5}(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot 32(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096584 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=3x-x^2$ and $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^2$. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186096584 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=3x-x^2$ and $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^2.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096584 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x-x^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^2\n$$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\n3x - x^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} \\\\\n3x - \\frac{3}{2}x^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n2x - x^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx(2 - x) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2$. So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2$.\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/5a/5a9fd40682646575ada14297ef7e1de766852f99dbdab986d5baa1fb5c2feb94.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=\\dfrac12 x^2$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=3x-x^2$.\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{a}^{b}x([upper] - [lower])dx \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{2}x([3x - x^{2}] - [\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}])dx \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{2}x(3x - \\frac{3}{2}x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 6π\\int_{0}^{2}x(x - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 6π\\int_{0}^{2}x(\\frac{2x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 3π\\int_{0}^{2}x(2x - x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 3π\\int_{0}^{2}(2x^{2} - x^{3})dx \\\\\n&= \\left.3π(\\frac{2x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{x^{4}}{4})\\right|_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3π[(\\frac{16}{3} - \\frac{16}{4}) - 0] \\\\\n&= 4π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186096584 1074 "First, let $f(x)=3x-x^2$ and $g(x)=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^2.$ \n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 3x-{x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ 3x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/5a/5a9fd40682646575ada14297ef7e1de766852f99dbdab986d5baa1fb5c2feb94.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=\\dfrac12 x^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=3x-x^2.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be calculated as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x([3x-{x}^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(3x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(\\frac{2x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\pi {(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\pi [(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{4})-0] \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541061 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541089 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541109 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541117 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541134 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541067 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541077 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541080 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541142 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186096873 1050 1020.0 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186097106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186097106 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y = 2x - \\frac{x^{2}}{2}$ and $y =-x + \\frac{5}{2}$. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186097106 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $y=-x+\\frac{5}{2}.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186097106 1074 "First, let $f(x)=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $g(x)=-x+\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} & =-x+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=1$ and $x=5.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=5.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](images/27/27919b5cbe7c0d8b7f7c58a9378f5aa3d30e5466c63b1b17d8a1b6af028ecf66.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=-x+\\frac{5}{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}x([2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]-[-x+\\frac{5}{2}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}x(-{x}^{2}+6x-5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}(-{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-5x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+2{x}^{3}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2})|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\pi [(-\\frac{625}{4}+250-\\frac{125}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{4}+2-\\frac{5}{2})] \\\\  & =\\pi [\\frac{125}{4}-(-\\frac{3}{4})] \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186097882 1064 "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$, as shown below. If we rotate this region $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we get a solid of revolution.\n\n![](images/e9/e9902ba13717e576f07a15396e6a78fd823a3ad92762688d7802cc7ac95849c0.png)\n\nWe can compute the volume of the solid using the shell method. We divide the interval from $x=a$ to $x=b$ on the $x$-axis to get vertical slices. Consider one such slice of width $\\Delta{x}$, as shown in the picture below (on the left). When this rectangle is rotated about the $y$-axis, it forms a solid shell (i.e., the skin of a cylinder).\n\n![](images/c7/c7be68342d347b8ef48e3cf2b8e20b88bedca594fc3d4846f03b5391ae8dbfc2.png)\n\nSince we assume that $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, the outer radius of the shell approximately equals the inner radius $x$. The thickness of the shell is $\\Delta{x}$ and its height is $y=f(x)$.\n\nImagine we cut this shell and unfold it to obtain a rectangular solid, as shown below. Its height is $y=f(x)$, its thickness is $\\Delta{x}$, and its length is $2\\pi x$ (the circumference of the shell).\n\n![](images/a4/a40f23b110ab24c55665784219b7013e587487f88d8d7857260458b9c7a20d2d.png)\n\nThe volume of the shell approximately equals the volume of the rectangular solid:\n\n$$\nV_{shell} \\mid \\approx V_{rec}; = 2πx \\cdot y \\cdot Δx; = 2πxf(x)Δx\n$$\n\nThe sum of all the shells will approximate the total volume we want. This approximation becomes more accurate as $\\Delta{x} \\to 0$. Therefore, the volume $V$ of the solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{a}^{b}xydx \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{a}^{b}xf(x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186097882 1064 "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b,$ as shown below. If we rotate this region $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we get a solid of revolution.\n\n![](images/e9/e9902ba13717e576f07a15396e6a78fd823a3ad92762688d7802cc7ac95849c0.png)\n\nWe can compute the volume of the solid using the shell method. We divide the interval from $x=a$ to $x=b$ on the $x$-axis to get vertical slices. Consider one such slice of width $\\Delta{x},$ as shown in the picture below (on the left). When this rectangle is rotated about the $y$-axis, it forms a solid shell (i.e., the skin of a cylinder).\n\n![](images/c7/c7be68342d347b8ef48e3cf2b8e20b88bedca594fc3d4846f03b5391ae8dbfc2.png)\n\nSince we assume that $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, the outer radius of the shell approximately equals the inner radius $x.$ The thickness of the shell is $\\Delta{x}$ and its height is $y=f(x)$.\n\nImagine we cut this shell and unfold it to obtain a rectangular solid, as shown below. Its height is $y=f(x)$, its thickness is $\\Delta{x},$ and its length is $2\\pi x$ (the circumference of the shell).\n\n![](images/a4/a40f23b110ab24c55665784219b7013e587487f88d8d7857260458b9c7a20d2d.png)\n\n \n\nThe volume of the shell approximately equals the volume of the rectangular solid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{shell}} & ≈{V}_{\\text{rec}} \\\\  & =2\\pi x\\cdot y\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\pi xf(x)Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of all the shells will approximate the total volume we want. This approximation becomes more accurate as $\\Delta{x} \\to 0$. Therefore, the volume $V$ of the solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186097883 1064 "Consider the region enclosed by the curves $y=f(x)$ on the top, $y=g(x)$ on the bottom, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$, as shown below. We assume that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq g(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in [a,b]$.\n\n![](images/37/372ece19360ca4f48a5ef48637e49d8f401ee2f1fd562f13c481c767a536bf6c.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we use the shell method as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{a}^{b}x([upper] - [lower])dx \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{a}^{b}x([f(x)] - [g(x)])dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186097883 1064 "Consider the region enclosed by the curves $y=f(x)$ on the top, $y=g(x)$ on the bottom, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b,$ as shown below. We assume that $f(x) \\geq g(x)$ for all $x \\in [a,b].$     \n\n![](images/37/372ece19360ca4f48a5ef48637e49d8f401ee2f1fd562f13c481c767a536bf6c.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we use the shell method as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([f(x)]-[g(x)])\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186097884 1064 "One of the advantages of using the shell method versus other methods is that the resulting integral can be much simpler to evaluate. This is often true when we want to rotate a curve of the form $y = f(x)$ about the $y$-axis.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the volume generated when the region below is rotated about the $y$-axis:\n\n![](images/ad/ad563abd1e7f4a413337dc9527d2c3703d4851e756f385a75797c6f5962cf51f.png)\n\nAccording to the shell method, the volume $V$ is given by the following straightforward integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}xf(x)dx \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}x(2x - 2x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}2x^{2} - 2x^{3}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now compare this to the disc method.\n\nTo determine the volume using the disc method, we would first have to express our curve as two functions of $y$, one for the right-hand side and one for the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n{x = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1 - 2y},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1 - 2y},\\end{vmatrix}x < \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/ee/ee7ef70a2a280c42ceea9dc6b8bdb3e3bafbfec0256350cbabde287d33d9884b.png)\n\nThe required volume equals the difference between the volumes generated when the right and left curves are rotated about the $y$-axis. Thus, according to the disc method, the required volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{0}^{1/2} \\left(\\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2y} \\right]^2 - \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2y} \\right]^2 \\right) \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nAs we can see, this integral is a lot more complicated than the one we obtained using the shell method!" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186097884 1064 "One of the advantages of using the shell method versus other methods is that the resulting integral can be much simpler to evaluate. This is often true when we want to rotate a curve of the form $y = f(x)$ about the $y$-axis.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the volume generated when the region below is rotated about the $y$-axis:\n\n![](images/ad/ad563abd1e7f4a413337dc9527d2c3703d4851e756f385a75797c6f5962cf51f.png)\n\nAccording to the shell method, the volume $V$ is given by the following straightforward integral: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}xf(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x(2x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now compare this to the disc method.\n\nTo determine the volume using the disc method, we would first have to express our curve as two functions of $y,$ one for the right-hand side and one for the left-hand side, as follows: \n\n![](images/a8/a841ed797699e8f20b494baa6ff888eb42ccd4715e17733e495405df5ca8c5dc.svg)\n\n![](images/ee/ee7ef70a2a280c42ceea9dc6b8bdb3e3bafbfec0256350cbabde287d33d9884b.png)\n\nThe required volume equals the difference between the volumes generated when the right and left curves are rotated about the $y$-axis. Thus, according to the disc method, the required volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{0}^{1/2} \\left( \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2y} \\right]^2 - \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2y} \\right]^2 \\right) \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nAs we can see, this integral is a lot more complicated than the one we obtained using the shell method!" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186101022 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541150 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186107604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540654 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186107604 1078 #edb/ref 17592186540654 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186107635 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107636 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186107635 1075 #edb/ref 17592186107637 13194139535913 17592186045