gned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186151937 1071 "Let $R = [1,3] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186152148 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541099 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152149 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541093 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152149 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541110 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541055 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541125 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541138 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541141 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152188 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541060 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152188 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541118 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152188 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541147 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541062 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541086 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541144 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541087 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541145 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541152 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152231 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541133 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152231 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541135 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152231 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541153 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152325 1050 660.0 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152363 1050 480.0 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152393 1071 "![](images/ee/ee1a59d15c7ad98ef2f0dd79d622c74f9fd419fa24db4212fe3bb35030e806a8.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x, y)$ defined on $R$. Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186152393 1071 "![](images/ee/ee1a59d15c7ad98ef2f0dd79d622c74f9fd419fa24db4212fe3bb35030e806a8.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152393 1074 "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\cdot {m}_{11}+2\\cdot {m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22} \\\\  & =2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152396 1071 "Let $R = [0, 3] \\cdot [0, 2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 6 - x - y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {0, 1, 2, 3}, P_{2} = {0, 2}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P)$." 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186152396 1071 "Let $R=[0,3]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=6-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152396 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/17/178108b256e55eaa5bd58c72f606fbed0aef774ff10a46e059c70abe93f8b341.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/dd/dd521e876abfc4313afb5beaf568c1d93cad670cfa912e210ed5d1548c51ec97.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/05/0515af2764a6ac27b2b69998ea4b99079dd7266a5989a161d01a6d25a670678e.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,2)=2 \\\\ {m}_{31} & =f(3,2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1=12.\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152710 1071 "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186152710 1071 "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186153349 1064 "In this lesson, we will describe a process for calculating a **lower bound** for the volume enclosed between a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane. \n\nAs an example, consider the rectangle $R$ in the $xy$-plane, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of the region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf7ef4752b0910b5544707eef97c854d8eeeedbc221a81c5fa631cf38b8b8d9a.png)\n\nNow consider the function $f(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2,$ defined over $R,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/34/34f7936b29e6a177ae0c831e36654dc5059514580a1947324a40d52320b83e5a.png)\n\n \n\nThe space underneath the surface and above the region $R$ defines a fixed volume $V.$ Our goal is to find a lower bound for $V.$ We do this by approximating this volume using rectangular solids. \n\nWe start by partitioning the $x$- and $y$-intervals of $R.$ Let's pick two subintervals of equal length for both $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/c3/c375b605ca6b7d7f2935218976f5e09db5ed2cfb64d811b1fe0a854d02646a36.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is now partitioned into $4$ rectangles:\n\n![](images/20/2026210e2cd58b2f2f7290a2d4f4186875903d146d02597787393727afd7f6a6.png)\n\nWe seek a *lower* bound for the volume $V.$ So, we let $m_{ij}$ be the *smallest* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We then define a rectangular solid in each subregion whose base is $R_{ij}$ and whose height is $m_{ij}.$ \n\nFor example, the rectangular solid corresponding to $R_{11}$ is given below. \n\n![](images/43/4324b984fb1056750f9bfceda58d57b2e0dbf1875ff21479d5ef93f088bfc50b.png)\n\n \n\nThe volume of this rectangular solid is\n\n$$\nm_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11})  = \\dfrac14m_{11}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the volumes of the rectangular solids formed by the partitions $R_{21}, R_{12},$ and $R_{22}$ respectively are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14m_{21}, \\qquad \\dfrac14m_{12}, \\qquad \\dfrac14m_{22}.\n$$\n\nDrawing all four rectangular solids, we get a volume that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/89/89d3706148d3f52096f94287c9cf4694b281bab18a8803bafe1b67d81e9b87fc.png)\n\n \n\nThe lower bound $L$ for our volume is the sum of the volumes of our four rectangular solids. This gives\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac14(m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12}+m_{22}).\n$$\n\nThe number $L$ is called the **lower Riemann sum with respect to the given partition**.\n\n**Note:** We don't yet know the values of $m_{ij},$ the height of each solid. We'll learn to figure these out a little later in the lesson. For now, we will just focus on writing $L$ as an expression of $m_{ij}.$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186153349 1064 "In this lesson, we will describe a process for calculating a **lower bound** for the volume enclosed between a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane. \n\nAs an example, consider the rectangle $R$ in the $xy$-plane, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of the region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf7ef4752b0910b5544707eef97c854d8eeeedbc221a81c5fa631cf38b8b8d9a.png)\n\nNow consider the function $f(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2,$ defined over $R,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/34/34f7936b29e6a177ae0c831e36654dc5059514580a1947324a40d52320b83e5a.png)\n\nThe space underneath the surface and above the region $R$ defines a fixed volume $V.$ Our goal is to find a lower bound for $V.$ We do this by approximating this volume using rectangular solids. \n\nWe start by partitioning the $x$- and $y$-intervals of $R.$ Let's pick two subintervals of equal length for both $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/c3/c375b605ca6b7d7f2935218976f5e09db5ed2cfb64d811b1fe0a854d02646a36.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is now partitioned into $4$ rectangles:\n\n![](images/20/2026210e2cd58b2f2f7290a2d4f4186875903d146d02597787393727afd7f6a6.png)\n\nWe seek a *lower* bound for the volume $V.$ So, we let $m_{ij}$ be the *smallest* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We then define a rectangular solid in each subregion whose base is $R_{ij}$ and whose height is $m_{ij}.$ \n\nFor example, the rectangular solid corresponding to $R_{11}$ is given below. \n\n![](images/43/4324b984fb1056750f9bfceda58d57b2e0dbf1875ff21479d5ef93f088bfc50b.png)\n\nThe volume of this rectangular solid is\n\n$$\nm_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11})  = \\dfrac14m_{11}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the volumes of the rectangular solids formed by the partitions $R_{21}, R_{12},$ and $R_{22}$ respectively are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14m_{21}, \\qquad \\dfrac14m_{12}, \\qquad \\dfrac14m_{22}.\n$$\n\nDrawing all four rectangular solids, we get a volume that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/89/89d3706148d3f52096f94287c9cf4694b281bab18a8803bafe1b67d81e9b87fc.png)\n\nThe lower bound $L$ for our volume is the sum of the volumes of our four rectangular solids. This gives\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac14(m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12}+m_{22}).\n$$\n\nThe number $L$ is called the **lower Riemann sum with respect to the given partition**.\n\n**Note:** We don't yet know the values of $m_{ij},$ the height of each solid. We'll learn to figure these out a little later in the lesson. For now, we will just focus on writing $L$ as an expression of $m_{ij}.$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186153874 1078 #edb/ref 17592186153879 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186157605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186157605 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$f(x) = \\frac{3}{4}(2x - x^{2}), 0 \\le x \\le 2$.\n\nCalculate $E[X^{3}]$." 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186157605 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3}{4}(2x-{x}^{2}),\\,0\\le x\\le 2.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186157605 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{10}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{5}}{10}-\\frac{{2}^{6}}{8})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{5}-8 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186158642 1078 #edb/ref 17592186158644 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186160820 1078 #edb/ref 17592186160825 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186161989 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541079 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186161989 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541136 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186161990 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541107 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186162062 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541084 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186162240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186162240 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X \\ge x) = p$, where $X∼χ^{2}(k)$. Given that the random variable $X∼χ^{2}(10)$, find the value of $x$ such that $P(X > x) = 0.950$.\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $1.344$ | $1.646$ | $2.180$ | $2.733$ | $3.490$ |\n| $9$ | $1.735$ | $2.088$ | $2.700$ | $3.325$ | $4.168$ |\n| $10$ | $2.156$ | $2.558$ | $3.247$ | $3.940$ | $4.865$ |" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186162240 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$  Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(10),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X>x)=0.950.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.344\\,$ | $\\,1.646\\,$ | $\\,2.180\\,$ | $\\,2.733\\,$ | $\\,3.490\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.735\\,$ | $\\,2.088\\,$ | $\\,2.700\\,$ | $\\,3.325\\,$ | $\\,4.168\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,2.156\\,$ | $\\,2.558\\,$ | $\\,3.247\\,$ | $\\,3.940\\,$ | $\\,4.865\\,$ |" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186162240 1074 "Since $X∼{χ}^{2}(10),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=10.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $10$ | $\\,2.156\\,$ | $\\,2.558\\,$ | $\\,3.247\\,$ | $\\,3.940\\,$ | $\\,4.865\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>3.940)=0.950.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=3.940.$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186162588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186162588 1071 "Consider the bilinear form $B(x, y)$, given by\n\n$B(x, y) = x_{1}y_{1} - 3x_{1}y_{3} - x_{2}y_{3} + x_{3}y_{3}$.\n\nEvaluate $B(x, y)$ when $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186162588 1071 "Consider the bilinear form $B(x,y),$ given by \n\n$$\nB(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-{x}_{2}{y}_{3}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate $B(x,y)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186162588 1074 "We are given that \n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSubstituting these into the bilinear expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-3{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-{x}_{2}{y}_{3}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2-3+0-4 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186163249 1064 "Let's recall the algorithm for diagonalizing a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A.$\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of unit orthogonal eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNote the following important points regarding step 2 of this process:\n\n- If $A$ has mutually distinct eigenvalues, the eigenvectors will automatically be orthogonal, so we only need to normalize them.\n\n- If $A$ has *repeated* eigenvalues, then the eigenvectors corresponding to these repeated eigenvalues *may not* be orthogonal. Therefore, we have to apply the Gram-Schmidt process to obtain orthogonal vectors.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/28/2807ddab01a673d01c385b0dcc1b77d007896f803376006e7f81f639930fecdc.svg)\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. Let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n![](images/95/95d0808a376ad679118ee26b9387cc3037862b74f12945db886a2ffd7738d525.svg)\nNotice that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n![](images/b4/b4aaec2c201e4ae0aff1054c57fdf4cc860dac1fa6bd38a9b58865b5306b8ce2.svg)\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ form an *orthogonal* basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n![](images/c9/c9a112d21b6868b583f521a73a5aa491a40ce46c365adf6ddc3f7eeb60c9f5fe.svg)\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/f1/f111684678ff06708513e20f22db25b4d6246dda2a8ce5f50b5905c9a13b0cbd.svg)" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186163249 1064 "Let's recall the algorithm for diagonalizing a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A.$\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of unit orthogonal eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNote the following important points regarding step 2 of this process:\n\n- If $A$ has mutually distinct eigenvalues, the eigenvectors will automatically be orthogonal, so we only need to normalize them.\n\n- If $A$ has *repeated* eigenvalues, then the eigenvectors corresponding to these repeated eigenvalues *may not* be orthogonal. Therefore, we have to apply the Gram-Schmidt process to obtain orthogonal vectors.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/28/2807ddab01a673d01c385b0dcc1b77d007896f803376006e7f81f639930fecdc.svg)\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. Let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n![](images/95/95d0808a376ad679118ee26b9387cc3037862b74f12945db886a2ffd7738d525.svg)\nNotice that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n![](images/b4/b4aaec2c201e4ae0aff1054c57fdf4cc860dac1fa6bd38a9b58865b5306b8ce2.svg)\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{1}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ form an *orthogonal* basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n![](images/c9/c9a112d21b6868b583f521a73a5aa491a40ce46c365adf6ddc3f7eeb60c9f5fe.svg)\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/f1/f111684678ff06708513e20f22db25b4d6246dda2a8ce5f50b5905c9a13b0cbd.svg)" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541126 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541130 13194139535913 1759