igned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186335783 1071 "![](images/5d/5d292a3598346bd05511c9c8979ba42845a88afe75a4740d8c4bdb9aa3968c87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 true] [17592186335783 1071 "![](images/5d/5d292a3598346bd05511c9c8979ba42845a88afe75a4740d8c4bdb9aa3968c87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" 13194139535913 17592186045417 false] [17592186335783 1074 "![](images/48/488ff32187c9a9a335e3782fb717242d899f622c43010662e28edcabe0e1bde3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{5}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{56}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9